          "**Type I error (alpha, usually 0.05)** - false positive; **type II error (beta, usually 0.2)** - false negative; **power = 1 - beta**, which rises with a larger sample, larger effect, smaller variability and higher alpha. [Park 28e]",
          "**Multiple testing** inflates type I error - 20 tests at 0.05 expect one false positive, so subgroups must be pre-specified and corrected (for example Bonferroni: alpha divided by number of tests). [Park 28e]",
          "**Choose a test with four questions**: type of outcome data, number of groups, paired or independent, normal or not - saying these aloud earns the mark (table below). [Mahajan 9e]",
